'''Christian Donald Laettner''' ({{IPAc-en|ˈ|l|eɪ|t|n|ər}}, {{respell|LAYT|nər}}; born August 17, 1969) is an American former professional [[basketball]] player. His college career for the [[Duke Blue Devils men's basketball|Duke Blue Devils]] is widely regarded as one of the best in [[National Collegiate Athletic Association]] (NCAA) history.{{efn|name=30great}} He was the star player on back-to-back Duke [[List of NCAA Men's Division I Basketball champions|National Championship teams]] of [[1990–91 Duke Blue Devils men's basketball team|1991]] and [[1991–92 Duke Blue Devils men's basketball team|1992]], and the [[List of U.S. men's college basketball national player of the year awards|NCAA player of the year]] in his senior year.<ref name="collegeHOF">[https://web.archive.org/web/20150218023958/http://www.collegebasketballexperience.com/inductees-search-page/111-l/552-christian-laettner Christian Laettner] at the National Collegiate Basketball Hall of Fame</ref> He is famous for [[The Shot (Duke–Kentucky)|his game-winning shot]] against [[1991–92 Kentucky Wildcats men's basketball team|Kentucky]] in the [[1992 NCAA Division I men's basketball tournament|1992 tournament]] and being polarizing amongst basketball fans.

Laettner was the only collegian selected for the [[1992 United States men's Olympic basketball team]], dubbed the "Dream Team", that won the gold medal at the [[1992 Summer Olympics]]. As a member of the "Dream Team", Laettner is enshrined in the [[Naismith Memorial Basketball Hall of Fame]], [[U.S. Olympic Hall of Fame]], and [[FIBA Hall of Fame]], while he is enshrined for his individual career in the [[College Basketball Hall of Fame]]. He was drafted third overall by the [[Minnesota Timberwolves]] in the [[1992 NBA draft]], then played 13 seasons in the [[National Basketball Association]] (NBA) for six teams, being named as an [[NBA All-Star Game|All-Star]] in 1997.

